{% block bodyContent %}
  <section>
    <h2>Introduction</h2>
    <p>In August 2025, our guidance stated that no artificial intelligence (AI) tool was approved for processing participant data. This guidance was revised (November 2025) to align with more flexible approaches currently being adopted across other government departments. In February 2026, the user-research guidance has been broadened to combine with guidance for designers.</p>
    <p>As NHS England, we are committed to enabling professional growth through experimentation, but safety and safeguarding must always come first.</p>
    <p>We have been evaluating a variety of AI tools during the last year, and in September 2026 we have released additional guidance on <a href="/artificial-intelligence/processing-research-data-with-ai-tools">Processing user research data with AI Tools</a>.</p>
    <p>Despite the guidance and tools being relaxed further, a key principle remains the same:</p>
    <p><span style="color:red;">Researchers remain fully accountable for the accuracy and integrity of their analysis and reporting. Any errors or biases resulting from the misuse of generative AI are their responsibility.</span></p>
    <p>A summary of key changes that apply to processing raw research data (updated September 2026):</p>
    <ol>
      <li>approved Microsoft tools may help create anonymised derivative research data;</li>
      <li>humans MUST approve it;</li>
      <li>Microsoft tools or GitHub Copilot can analyse human-approved anonymised derivative research data;</li>
      <li>all final outputs must be human-reviewed before publication.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Examples where using AI is NOT allowed for research purposes</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Do not input official or sensitive NHS England data into (non-corporate approved) AI tools.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Unannounced policies.</li>
          <li>Internal communications.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><i>Reason:</i> This prevents disclosure of confidential information outside the organisation.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Do not use AI to simulate research participants (e.g. in interviews, usability testing, surveys, card sorting, AB testing, personas, etc.)</strong></p>
        <p>AI simulations cannot replace real user insights and are likely to exacerbate health inequalities. AI-generated data are likely biased and lack grounding in research evidence. They risk reinforcing stereotypes, misinterpreting user needs and exclude insights from people who face the most barriers to accessing our services.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Humans bring lived experience, emotions and context that shape their behaviour. AI generates responses based on patterns in data it was trained on. It can approximate behaviour, but it doesn’t experience things.</li>
          <li>Humans interpret meaning based on personal, cultural and situational context. AI can simulate context if explicitly described. But otherwise, it might miss cues and overgeneralise.</li>
          <li>Human participants bring diverse perspectives shaped by their identities, environments and histories. AI can simulate diversity, but it’s limited by the data it was trained on and the assumptions included into its prompts.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><i>Reasons:</i> Simulated responses are not evidence-based and can mislead design decisions. Human participants may disagree, challenge assumptions or describe unexpected experiences. AI models often exhibit sycophantic behaviour, producing responses that appear helpful or agreeable rather than accurately reflecting real-world experiences.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><strong>Do not use AI to make ethical or safeguarding decisions that require human judgement.</strong></p>
        <p>Human oversight is essential for decisions involving participant or patient safety and wellbeing. AI cannot fully understand context, nuance, or moral implications.</p>
        <p><i>Reason:</i> Relying on AI for these decisions risks overlooking subtle indicators of harm or bias, which could lead to unethical outcomes or safeguarding failures. Always involve a qualified person in making these judgments.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Examples where AI is allowed for research purposes</h2>
    <p>What you can and cannot do with AI depends significantly on the task. In this section, we outline practical examples for two key stages: planning user research and analysing user research.</p>
    <p>Please read both carefully, as the rules differ. In particular, pay close attention to the guidance on analysis—this is where the highest risks lie, and not all uses are permitted. Performance will vary across different AI tools and tasks.</p>
    <p>There is also new guidance on UR data storage and AI tools usage for user research: <a href="/artificial-intelligence/processing-research-data-with-ai-tools">Processing user research data with AI Tools</a>.</p>

    <article>
      <h3>Planning research</h3>
      <p>Below are examples of planning tasks where AI can assist. This is not an exhaustive list:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Refining research plans</strong>: improve problem statements, aims, objectives, and assumptions.</li>
        <li><strong>Improving discussion guides</strong>: refine questions or prompts for interviews and workshops.</li>
        <li><strong>Enhancing research materials</strong>: review and improve participant information sheets, consent forms, posters, and flyers.</li>
        <li><strong>Proofreading communications</strong>: check spelling, grammar and tone in emails to participants and recruiters.</li>
        <li><strong>Planning workshops</strong>: suggest timings, icebreakers, and clear instructions for activities.</li>
        <li><strong>Improving survey questions</strong>: refine wording for clarity and neutrality.</li>
        <li><strong>Gathering background information</strong>: use AI to get an overview of a topic—but proceed with caution. AI-generated content may include inaccuracies or fabricated details. If you use AI for this purpose, choose tools that provide verifiable sources and always cross-check the information.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h3>Analysing research</h3>
      <p>Researchers are now permitted to use Microsoft 365 Copilot to assist with the analysis of user research data, including personal data.</p>
      <p>Please ensure you are logged into Copilot using your NHS.net Connect account. This enterprise version of Copilot is not the same as the copilot you can access freely on your personal devices.</p>
      <p><span style="color:red;">Copilot should never replace human judgment, do not use it for making decisions like which user needs are most important, or which features should we recommend. As an NHS England researcher, you remain fully accountable for the outputs of the research.</span></p>

      <p>More details on how Copilot works can be found on <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/copilot/microsoft-365/microsoft-365-copilot-architecture" target="_blank">Microsoft Learn.</a> In summary, when you use Microsoft 365 Copilot:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your data stays within the Microsoft 365 boundary.</li>
        <li>Your data is secured based on existing security, compliance and privacy policies already deployed by your organisation.</li>
        <li>Prompts, responses and data accessed through Microsoft Graph aren’t used to train foundation LLMs.</li>
        <li>Microsoft 365 Copilot uses Azure OpenAI services for processing, not OpenAI’s publicly available services. Azure OpenAI doesn't cache customer content and Copilot modified prompts for Microsoft 365 Copilot.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Before you start using Copilot please read the <a href="https://support.nhs.net/knowledge-base/m365-copilot-acceptable-use-policy/#2-document-information-and-downloads" target="_blank">M365 Copilot Acceptable Use Policy</a>.</p>

      <h4>How to use Copilot to assist with analysis?</h4>
      <p>You can use Copilot for analysis only with anonymised data and under the following conditions:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Review process</strong>
          <ul>
            <li>Researchers must review all outputs in full.</li>
            <li>Researchers should never rely on AI-generated outputs without checking them thoroughly.</li>
            <li>For large survey data sets it may not be feasible for a researcher to check the output of the AI tool analysis. Therefore researchers should make an informed decision in the research planning stages whether the perceived benefits are worth it.</li>
            <li>Validate all findings, insights and quotes against the original data to ensure accuracy and context.</li>
            <li>Never publish any outputs that have been written 100% by AI tools.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Disclaimer in research reports</strong>
          <p>If AI has been used to assist with analysis and write-up of the report, include a disclaimer at the beginning of the report stating:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Which AI tool was used (including version)</li>
            <li>How it was used, e.g., to identify key findings and insights, search for quotes, check grammar etc. Example text: <i>“This report was generated with the help of [insert tool and model version here] and carefully reviewed by our UCD team for accuracy and appropriateness. AI was used to [insert brief description here].”</i></li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Participant consent and transparency</strong>
          <p>To ensure ethical and transparent use of AI in research analysis:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Include a clear statement about AI use.</strong> Recommended wording:<br><i>“AI may be used to support the user research analysis process. None of your personal data will ever be imported into an AI tool. Only anonymised data (i.e. survey responses or transcripts) will be used with AI. All analysis using AI will be reviewed and assessed for accuracy by a member of staff."</i></li>
            <li><strong>Use updated templates.</strong> Standard participant information sheet template will be updated shortly to reflect these additions.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>As per <a href="https://www.mrs.org.uk/pdf/AI+RelatedTechnologies_MRSGuidance.pdf" target="_blank">Market Research Society guidance</a> a decision was made that we should be transparent about AI use so that people:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Are aware that AI may be used</li>
            <li>Know how it may be used</li>
            <li>Can ask questions about how it is used</li>
          </ul>
          <p>However, participants don’t need to consent to AI being used to analyse their data.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h4>You should be very cautious using AI for these parts of the research analysis process:</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Recommendations:</strong> Recommendations for product or service changes must be written by the researcher or UCD team members. Even if AI assisted with analysis, it may lack critical context and team knowledge. Writing recommendations is one of the most important responsibilities of a researcher—outsourcing this task would be highly risky.</li>
        <li><strong>Personas:</strong> Unless the AI tool you are using can provide exact citations for the sources behind its insights, creating personas with it is risky because it becomes difficult to verify         how they were generated and whether they embed biases. In general, views on the value of personas in user research are mixed. While some teams find them useful, others question their effectiveness.</li>
              </ul>
            </article>
